//? Title: Mann–Whitney, Siegel–Tukey and Ansari–Bradley
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Two simulated groups have the same center and different spread. Each
test sees the same observations. The scale tests score observations from
the outside of the pooled sample inward.
)

> Data and analysis

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rank_scale_tests.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace sp = dm::stats;
using dm::graphics::RPlot;

int main() {
    dm::set_seed(17);
    const auto wide = dm::rnorm(0, 2.6, 48);
    const auto narrow = dm::rnorm(0, 0.75, 48);
    const auto u = sp::mann_whitney_test(wide, narrow);
    const auto siegel = sp::siegel_tukey_test(wide, narrow, sp::Alternative::Greater);
    const auto ansari = sp::ansari_bradley_test(wide, narrow, sp::Alternative::Greater);
    std::print("| Method | Statistic | p-value |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto* test : {&u, &siegel, &ansari})
        std::print("| {} | {:.1f} | {:.4g} |\n", test->method, test->statistic, test->p_value);
    std::print("\nEstimated probability of superiority for the wide group: {:.3f}.\n", u.estimate1);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    RPlot::boxplot({wide.values(), narrow.values()}, {"Wide", "Narrow"})
        .title("Same population center, different spread").y_label("Response")
        .save_svg("build/rank_scale_tests.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Method | Statistic | p-value |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Wilcoxon rank sum test with continuity correction | 1167.0 | 0.9154 |
| Siegel-Tukey scale test (normal approximation) | 1605.0 | 5.858e-08 |
| Ansari-Bradley scale test (normal approximation) | 815.0 | 6.069e-08 |

Estimated probability of superiority for the wide group: 0.507.
\image(figures/rank_scale_tests.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Observed spreads and centers of the two simulated groups.)
\alttext(Observed spreads and centers of the two simulated groups.)

> Interpretation

Mann–Whitney U estimates the chance that a wide-group draw exceeds a
narrow-group draw, counting a tie as one-half. The two scale tests focus
on whether one sample occupies more of the extremes. A scale interpretation
assumes a common center and similar shapes. Here both scale tests use normal
approximations; the scale direction in the API is the direction of spread,
even though the wide group's scores tend to be smaller.

> References

- [R Wilcoxon rank-sum test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/wilcox.test.html]
- [R Ansari–Bradley test|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ansari.test.html]
- [NIST Siegel–Tukey test|https://itl.nist.gov/div898/software/dataplot/refman1/auxillar/siegel.htm]
